1
00:00:10,135 --> 00:00:13,930
[rocket engine whooshing] 

2
00:00:16,433 --> 00:00:19,019
[soldiers singing indistinctly]

3
00:00:21,521 --> 00:00:22,981
[cell phone buzzing, dinging]

4
00:00:31,906 --> 00:00:35,035
POTUS is still mad
about the whole India thing.

5
00:00:35,118 --> 00:00:38,913
So, as punishment, he wants us 
to partner with private industry.

6
00:00:38,997 --> 00:00:43,460
Specifically, he wants us
to use this tech guru's new fuel

7
00:00:43,543 --> 00:00:45,295
in the lunar satellite launch.

8
00:00:45,545 --> 00:00:46,546
Not her.

9
00:00:48,006 --> 00:00:49,966
Ed... Edison Jaymes? 
Are you kidding me?

10
00:00:50,050 --> 00:00:53,553
No. She's gonna be here in a few minutes
to inspect the base

11
00:00:53,928 --> 00:00:56,681
and make sure
that we are worthy of her product.

12
00:00:56,765 --> 00:00:57,724
I read about that.

13
00:00:57,807 --> 00:01:01,936
It's supposedly half as heavy
as normal fuel, and no pollutants.

14
00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:03,730
I mean, that would be a game changer.

15
00:01:03,813 --> 00:01:07,358
God, I am so sick of people
fawning over these young tech moguls.

16
00:01:07,442 --> 00:01:09,194
The government is plenty innovative.

17
00:01:09,277 --> 00:01:11,988
Did you know that the US Postal Service

18
00:01:12,072 --> 00:01:14,157
issues a new Christmas stamp
every Christmas

19
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:16,409
and has done so for the past 200 years?

20
00:01:16,826 --> 00:01:19,496
Mark, I don't think the Postal Service
still exists.

21
00:01:20,747 --> 00:01:22,540
-Well, I think they do.
-No.

22
00:01:22,624 --> 00:01:24,959
Where do you think our funding came from?

23
00:01:29,172 --> 00:01:30,298
Hmm. 

24
00:01:33,885 --> 00:01:34,928
She's late.

25
00:01:35,178 --> 00:01:36,471
She's too rich to be late.

26
00:01:36,554 --> 00:01:38,181
Rich people are such babies.

27
00:01:44,354 --> 00:01:45,438
[Mark] There she is.

28
00:01:45,772 --> 00:01:49,275
Well, jeans 
and $100,000 worth of diamonds.

29
00:01:49,359 --> 00:01:50,401
That's a good look.

30
00:01:50,485 --> 00:01:51,903
It's a confusing look.

31
00:01:51,986 --> 00:01:55,657
I don't know whether she's going
to Cinderella's ball or a pig roast.

32
00:01:56,366 --> 00:01:58,284
Fuck. Fuck me.

33
00:01:58,368 --> 00:02:00,161
[Mark] You bite the inside of your mouth?
I've done that.

34
00:02:00,245 --> 00:02:02,372
No. That's Hannah Howard.

35
00:02:02,789 --> 00:02:04,290
We came up in the PR world together.

36
00:02:04,374 --> 00:02:07,585
She single-handedly orchestrated
my dismissal from American Apparel

37
00:02:07,669 --> 00:02:10,046
by leaking my text messages.

38
00:02:10,505 --> 00:02:13,842
She is by far the worst person
I have ever met in my entire life.

39
00:02:14,259 --> 00:02:15,135
Hannah!

40
00:02:15,218 --> 00:02:16,845
Oh, my God, I'm coming down.

41
00:02:16,928 --> 00:02:18,012
You look amazing!

42
00:02:18,388 --> 00:02:19,514
[Mallory] Ms. Jaymes?

43
00:02:22,684 --> 00:02:24,394
Here he is. [chuckles] 

44
00:02:24,561 --> 00:02:25,520
Nice to meet you.

45
00:02:25,603 --> 00:02:26,855
-General Naird.
-Welcome to Space--

46
00:02:26,938 --> 00:02:28,857
-Ah-ah.
-[camera clicks]

47
00:02:29,649 --> 00:02:30,650
Good to tweet?

48
00:02:31,359 --> 00:02:33,069
-Yes, with the usual adjustments.
-Of course.

49
00:02:33,778 --> 00:02:36,197
When POTUS called me
and asked me to help you guys out,

50
00:02:36,281 --> 00:02:37,198
I was stoked.

51
00:02:37,615 --> 00:02:40,535
I was stoked as well. And... amped.

52
00:02:40,952 --> 00:02:45,290
Yeah. Not just for our country,
but my rocket fuel needs customers.

53
00:02:45,373 --> 00:02:48,084
And you'll have to forgive me
if I sound like a woman on a mission,

54
00:02:48,918 --> 00:02:49,836
but I am one.

55
00:02:50,962 --> 00:02:54,924
You see, my fuel
makes your rocket burn more cleanly.

56
00:02:55,842 --> 00:02:56,801
Well done.

57
00:02:57,552 --> 00:02:58,636
Oh, please.

58
00:03:00,263 --> 00:03:02,140
[Fred on phone] 
Have you made any new friends yet?

59
00:03:02,223 --> 00:03:03,308
No, Grandpa.

60
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:05,310
I still have my old friends in DC.

61
00:03:05,393 --> 00:03:06,394
No, you don't.

62
00:03:06,811 --> 00:03:08,771
People move on fast at your age.

63
00:03:09,439 --> 00:03:12,525
I can't name one friend
I had in high school.

64
00:03:12,609 --> 00:03:14,569
Oh, I'm guessing there was an Al.

65
00:03:15,153 --> 00:03:16,863
-There were a lot of Als back then.
-[grunting] 

66
00:03:17,363 --> 00:03:18,990
Most of them died during the war, though.

67
00:03:19,073 --> 00:03:20,200
[moaning] 

68
00:03:20,283 --> 00:03:21,868
That's why there's no more Als.

69
00:03:22,869 --> 00:03:23,703
Cool.

70
00:03:24,287 --> 00:03:25,538
[Fred] Hey, put your mom on.

71
00:03:26,372 --> 00:03:28,833
My phone's at one percent,
and Mom's in prison.

72
00:03:28,917 --> 00:03:30,043
Oh, my gosh.

73
00:03:30,293 --> 00:03:32,337
Darling,
you've got to keep your phone char--

74
00:03:35,298 --> 00:03:36,132
Great.

75
00:04:02,742 --> 00:04:03,701
Nice.

76
00:04:04,452 --> 00:04:05,370
Very...

77
00:04:06,496 --> 00:04:07,372
very...

78
00:04:08,414 --> 00:04:09,749
nice.

79
00:04:24,639 --> 00:04:26,516
[exhales] 

80
00:04:29,852 --> 00:04:31,437
Ooh. Fancy.

81
00:04:31,521 --> 00:04:32,563
-[knocking]
-Ah!

82
00:04:32,647 --> 00:04:34,274
License and registration, miss!

83
00:04:35,441 --> 00:04:36,567
-[laughs]
-Jesus Christ!

84
00:04:36,651 --> 00:04:38,444
Duncan, you scared the shit out of me!

85
00:04:38,528 --> 00:04:39,821
Oh, I'm sorry.

86
00:04:40,238 --> 00:04:42,073
-Should I get rags or paper towels?
-Oh, no.

87
00:04:42,156 --> 00:04:44,409
-My mom hates wasting paper towels...
-Oh, no, no.

88
00:04:44,492 --> 00:04:46,703
...but folks don't sell rags in Colorado--

89
00:04:46,786 --> 00:04:48,079
Oh, just move. Move, move, move!

90
00:04:48,162 --> 00:04:49,914
Just go. Go, go, go!

91
00:04:51,332 --> 00:04:55,169
This is a sample
of the treatment I recently developed

92
00:04:55,253 --> 00:04:58,214
for methicillin-resistant pathogens
in space.

93
00:04:58,298 --> 00:05:02,010
Bleh. God, that thing. Six months
and a bunch of rats over budget.

94
00:05:02,885 --> 00:05:04,721
At the biotech arm of Edison Jaymes,

95
00:05:04,804 --> 00:05:07,682
it would have taken us half the time
and a quarter of the rats.

96
00:05:08,099 --> 00:05:11,185
Well, always said
we spend way too much money on the rats.

97
00:05:11,269 --> 00:05:12,854
Well, it didn't take that long.

98
00:05:13,396 --> 00:05:16,816
I did invent a medicine, after all.

99
00:05:17,233 --> 00:05:19,485
Thank you for signing these NDAs.
Standard procedure.

100
00:05:19,569 --> 00:05:22,280
Thank you for signing these NDAs.
You get it. [laughs] 

101
00:05:22,363 --> 00:05:24,240
You know what an NDA is,
don't you, Scarapiducci?

102
00:05:24,324 --> 00:05:27,285
So cute. You know what?
Ours don't even need to be signed.

103
00:05:27,368 --> 00:05:30,413
-You sign them just by being here.
-Hmm. Never heard of that.

104
00:05:30,496 --> 00:05:33,583
What you don't know about this place
could fill a giant underground silo

105
00:05:33,666 --> 00:05:35,001
that you don't even know exists.

106
00:05:35,084 --> 00:05:36,711
Mm. Is that where they keep your office?

107
00:05:37,378 --> 00:05:38,212
No.

108
00:05:41,174 --> 00:05:43,593
Hey!
I heard you know some shit about plants.

109
00:05:43,843 --> 00:05:44,927
Hmm. Let's see.

110
00:05:45,011 --> 00:05:47,138
I'm an astrobotanist
with an advanced degree

111
00:05:47,221 --> 00:05:50,391
in cross-pollination
and dry terrain root systems.

112
00:05:50,475 --> 00:05:51,309
What does that mean?

113
00:05:51,893 --> 00:05:53,811
I know some shit about plants.

114
00:05:54,103 --> 00:05:55,146
Sorry. Who are you?

115
00:05:55,229 --> 00:05:56,773
I'm Captain Angela Ali.

116
00:05:58,316 --> 00:05:59,859
I wanna learn astrobotany.

117
00:06:00,359 --> 00:06:01,402
For fun.

118
00:06:01,486 --> 00:06:03,738
Well, I'm not a science tutor.

119
00:06:04,113 --> 00:06:05,823
Well, I'll pay you 50 bucks an hour.

120
00:06:06,532 --> 00:06:08,659
I could do every day from noon to one.

121
00:06:08,743 --> 00:06:11,704
Great. Meet me here. Please be organized,
be prepped, and be early.

122
00:06:11,788 --> 00:06:14,707
I really don't wanna pay for, like,
"Hi, how are you, weather" bullshit.

123
00:06:14,791 --> 00:06:18,252
Cool! I haven't started,
and I already wanna quit.

124
00:06:18,336 --> 00:06:19,921
So glad I made this choice.

125
00:06:20,004 --> 00:06:20,838
Yeah.

126
00:06:20,922 --> 00:06:21,756
Me too.

127
00:06:24,717 --> 00:06:25,802
[controlled exhale]

128
00:06:25,885 --> 00:06:26,719
[Edison] Wow.

129
00:06:27,261 --> 00:06:29,305
Which one of these awards
means the most to you?

130
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:32,141
Bronze Star.

131
00:06:32,850 --> 00:06:36,896
I was shot down over Bosnia,
survived on worms for six days.

132
00:06:36,979 --> 00:06:37,814
Oh, I did that.

133
00:06:37,897 --> 00:06:39,607
-On an episode of Bear Grylls.
-Oh. 

134
00:06:39,690 --> 00:06:41,234
Yeah. Very keto.

135
00:06:41,651 --> 00:06:42,527
Okay.

136
00:06:44,362 --> 00:06:48,199
I used a sponge to collect rainwater
and store it in plastic bags.

137
00:06:49,158 --> 00:06:51,035
Where did you get a sponge
and plastic bags?

138
00:06:51,536 --> 00:06:52,578
Piddle pack.

139
00:06:53,454 --> 00:06:55,373
Pilots use them to relieve themselves.

140
00:06:56,082 --> 00:06:57,125
Sexually.

141
00:06:57,417 --> 00:06:58,251
[sighs]

142
00:06:58,334 --> 00:07:01,504
Mmm, no, no. Like going to the bathroom.

143
00:07:02,713 --> 00:07:03,840
Oh, my God.

144
00:07:05,133 --> 00:07:07,051
General Mark Naird,
do you know what you are?

145
00:07:07,135 --> 00:07:08,136
No, no, no, no.

146
00:07:08,219 --> 00:07:09,387
I am not a deviant.

147
00:07:09,470 --> 00:07:12,807
I didn't pee on the sponge and then use
that same sponge to collect rainwater.

148
00:07:12,890 --> 00:07:15,560
-That's not my thing.
-Right. No. No. 

149
00:07:16,310 --> 00:07:18,521
You are an innovator!

150
00:07:18,729 --> 00:07:20,148
[chuckles] Oh, okay.

151
00:07:20,231 --> 00:07:21,232
-You are.
-Yeah.

152
00:07:21,315 --> 00:07:22,442
-Well...
-Think about it.

153
00:07:22,525 --> 00:07:23,818
That was very MacGyver.

154
00:07:24,235 --> 00:07:26,362
If that's not innovation,
then I don't even know what is.

155
00:07:26,445 --> 00:07:29,282
I wouldn't really call it innovation.
It was just something that I did.

156
00:07:29,365 --> 00:07:30,658
It was just using the tools

157
00:07:30,741 --> 00:07:32,535
-that I had at my disposal.
-Come on.

158
00:07:33,035 --> 00:07:34,036
[clears throat] 

159
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:35,580
I have an idea, from time to time.

160
00:07:35,663 --> 00:07:39,250
-I had a feeling.
-I am not a tech whiz like you,

161
00:07:39,333 --> 00:07:41,085
but I think of some things.

162
00:07:41,169 --> 00:07:43,588
-Tell me. Tell me! 
-No. No, they're silly.

163
00:07:44,547 --> 00:07:45,465
I wanna hear.

164
00:07:46,799 --> 00:07:47,758
Okay.

165
00:07:49,135 --> 00:07:51,512
So you know how people shake hands
all over the world?

166
00:07:51,596 --> 00:07:53,264
-Mm-hmm.
-It's a universal thing.

167
00:07:53,347 --> 00:07:56,684
It is a sign of respect, decency.

168
00:07:57,518 --> 00:07:59,562
Yeah. It's a normal human handshake.

169
00:07:59,645 --> 00:08:02,064
Exactly. So imagine a device,

170
00:08:02,440 --> 00:08:05,818
like a mannequin, with a shakable arm.

171
00:08:06,235 --> 00:08:09,614
And you put one in Times Square.
You put on in Tiananmen Square.

172
00:08:09,697 --> 00:08:11,991
It's all linked up technologically.

173
00:08:12,074 --> 00:08:13,701
You've got video screens.

174
00:08:13,784 --> 00:08:16,454
You can shake the hand
of someone in China.

175
00:08:20,791 --> 00:08:22,668
It's brilliant.

176
00:08:24,003 --> 00:08:26,631
-Bringing strangers together.
-That's exactly right!

177
00:08:26,797 --> 00:08:28,299
-Mm-hmm.
-That's what I've been saying!

178
00:08:28,382 --> 00:08:30,927
-I love it.
-Shaking hands across the world.

179
00:08:31,552 --> 00:08:32,845
Will that be its name?

180
00:08:33,679 --> 00:08:34,639
Yes.

181
00:08:35,056 --> 00:08:36,015
It's good.

182
00:08:36,390 --> 00:08:39,352
So-- And inside the booth,
there's an attendant.

183
00:08:39,435 --> 00:08:42,855
That precludes
any sort of sexual shenanigans going on.

184
00:08:42,939 --> 00:08:43,940
Mmm.

185
00:08:44,023 --> 00:08:45,399
And you don't have to tip the attendant

186
00:08:45,483 --> 00:08:47,485
because it's part
of the cost of admission.

187
00:08:47,568 --> 00:08:48,444
It's all covered.

188
00:08:48,528 --> 00:08:49,403
Okay.

189
00:08:49,487 --> 00:08:52,406
It sounds silly when you just say it
to people, but it's...

190
00:08:52,490 --> 00:08:53,533
No! No, it does not.

191
00:08:53,616 --> 00:08:55,952
Mark, this is your vision.

192
00:08:56,035 --> 00:08:57,328
I don't want you to shame it.

193
00:08:57,411 --> 00:08:58,788
-[chuckling]
-You believe in it,

194
00:08:59,622 --> 00:09:01,290
and you will change the world.

195
00:09:02,833 --> 00:09:03,668
Well... 

196
00:09:03,751 --> 00:09:04,669
Oh. 

197
00:09:05,753 --> 00:09:06,796
Wow.

198
00:09:07,004 --> 00:09:08,923
[chuckling] 

199
00:09:09,048 --> 00:09:11,217
Okay. We need to get our stories straight.

200
00:09:11,884 --> 00:09:14,178
The guy who spilled the soft serve

201
00:09:14,262 --> 00:09:17,974
was a rich white guy
in a "Where's Waldo" T-shirt.

202
00:09:18,391 --> 00:09:21,143
See?
That's so specific, people will buy it.

203
00:09:21,394 --> 00:09:23,479
I don't know. I can't picture it.

204
00:09:24,230 --> 00:09:26,107
You can't picture people believing it?

205
00:09:26,190 --> 00:09:27,984
No, I can't picture
what you're describing.

206
00:09:28,067 --> 00:09:29,694
I don't have that ability.

207
00:09:31,362 --> 00:09:32,321
Okay.

208
00:09:32,863 --> 00:09:33,864
Close your eyes.

209
00:09:35,574 --> 00:09:37,326
Ronald McDonald on a surfboard.

210
00:09:38,286 --> 00:09:39,203
What do you see?

211
00:09:39,829 --> 00:09:42,456
Nothing.
It's just like staring at a blank page.

212
00:09:43,249 --> 00:09:44,625
Oh, my God.

213
00:09:45,376 --> 00:09:47,837
You have no imagination. That's...

214
00:09:48,546 --> 00:09:49,755
That's adorable.

215
00:09:50,881 --> 00:09:52,758
Hey, it's a real condition.

216
00:09:52,842 --> 00:09:54,176
-Aphantasia.
-Oh.

217
00:09:54,260 --> 00:09:56,512
Yes. Well, way to sell yourself.

218
00:09:56,846 --> 00:09:58,681
So, what are your strengths?

219
00:09:58,764 --> 00:10:00,766
Oh, that is one of my strengths.

220
00:10:00,850 --> 00:10:02,602
I can stare at a cereal box.

221
00:10:02,685 --> 00:10:04,645
That's like watching HBO to me.

222
00:10:07,481 --> 00:10:11,736
I can't wait to talk to you
about the nuts and bolts of your fuel,

223
00:10:11,819 --> 00:10:13,362
the actual chemistry.

224
00:10:13,446 --> 00:10:14,864
Patience, Dr. Mallory.

225
00:10:14,947 --> 00:10:16,949
I'm about to answer all of your questions.

226
00:10:17,491 --> 00:10:20,703
In fact,
may I invite you to a little barbecue?

227
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:28,502
-[clears throat] Your phone.
-No, thank you.

228
00:10:28,878 --> 00:10:30,171
You have to put it in the secure case,

229
00:10:30,254 --> 00:10:32,173
which will open again
after the demo is over.

230
00:10:32,256 --> 00:10:33,799
No phones is part of the NDA you signed.

231
00:10:33,883 --> 00:10:35,217
Yeah, no,
I don't think I'll be doing that,

232
00:10:35,301 --> 00:10:37,637
because the last time
I handed you my phone,

233
00:10:37,720 --> 00:10:40,931
I wasn't allowed within 300 feet
of my own fucking office.

234
00:10:41,015 --> 00:10:43,559
Are you still holding a grudge?
Don't be petulant, Anthony.

235
00:10:43,642 --> 00:10:45,895
"Don't be petulant, Anthony."
You're a goblin.

236
00:10:46,270 --> 00:10:47,271
[clears throat] 

237
00:10:47,688 --> 00:10:49,607
-What? What are you doing?
-The ankle.

238
00:10:50,066 --> 00:10:51,776
-The holster on your ankle...
-This is so--

239
00:10:51,859 --> 00:10:53,569
-...that you hand-stitched yourself.
-This is stupid. 

240
00:10:53,653 --> 00:10:55,321
You are pathetic. Jesus Christ.

241
00:10:55,946 --> 00:10:57,239
For generations,

242
00:10:57,323 --> 00:11:00,534
chemists have looked to the stars
and asked a question:

243
00:11:01,577 --> 00:11:05,623
Can a fuel be created
that consumes its own exhaust?

244
00:11:06,582 --> 00:11:08,084
Those chemists were men,

245
00:11:08,542 --> 00:11:10,044
and the answer was no.

246
00:11:10,711 --> 00:11:11,962
And then I came along.

247
00:11:12,338 --> 00:11:16,092
Ladies and gentlemen,
presenting Skinny Fuel...

248
00:11:16,759 --> 00:11:18,469
a rosé colored rocket fuel

249
00:11:18,552 --> 00:11:23,641
cheaper, lighter, and cleaner
than any fuel ever created.

250
00:11:24,433 --> 00:11:27,144
I can't help but be impressed.

251
00:11:27,561 --> 00:11:30,481
-Thank you.
-But it's just that NASA astrochemists

252
00:11:30,564 --> 00:11:32,983
have been trying to concoct a solution

253
00:11:33,067 --> 00:11:35,486
with the same end result for years,

254
00:11:35,569 --> 00:11:39,115
